<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.9" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Recurrent sudden spells since adolescent head injury, cause under evaluation</content>. Brief spells that end abruptly with full recovery and no convulsive or post-ictal features favor sleep attacks over focal seizures. Routine EEG today shows intermittent left frontal slowing over the old injury but no epileptiform discharges; MRI shows an old depressed left frontal skull defect with small underlying encephalomalacia and normal hippocampi. [S]<list><item>Ambulatory EEG with video to capture typical spells.</item><item>Overnight polysomnography followed by a multiple sleep latency test.</item><item>No antiseizure medicine unless the spells prove epileptic.</item><item>Safety advice for sudden sleep: sit down at the first sign of sleepiness; she does not drive.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Chronic post-traumatic headache (G44.329)</content>. Headache on most days since the injury, worsened by analgesic overuse. Blood pressure 140/84 mmHg. A non-sedating preventive is preferred. [S]<list><item>Start candesartan 8 mg daily for headache prevention; it also lowers her mildly raised blood pressure.</item><item>Potassium and creatinine in four weeks, before increasing the dose.</item><item>Continue the headache diary.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Medication-overuse headache, withdrawal under way (G44.40)</content>. Two weeks without ibuprofen; the expected withdrawal worsening has passed. [S]<list><item>Acetaminophen on no more than 2 days a week; no NSAIDs.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Sequelae of adolescent traumatic brain injury (S06.9XAS)</content>. Old left frontal skull defect with small encephalomalacia on MRI, consistent with the historical injury; no new lesion. [S]<list><item>No further imaging unless new neurological signs appear.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Protocol monitoring: items the monitoring protocol calls for at this visit. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Measure</th><th>Interval</th><th>Status</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Referral</td><td>Neurology evaluation of possible seizures</td><td>After a first or uncertain seizure</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Seen today</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Routine EEG</td><td>After a possible seizure</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Intermittent left frontal slowing; no epileptiform discharges</td></tr><tr><td>Imaging</td><td>MRI of the brain</td><td>After a possible seizure</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Old depressed left frontal skull defect with small encephalomalacia; normal hippocampi; no tumor</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Ambulatory EEG with video to capture typical spells</td><td>When routine EEG is not diagnostic</td><td>Ordered</td><td>Booked for early next month</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Overnight polysomnography and multiple sleep latency test</td><td>For excessive daytime sleepiness</td><td>Ordered</td><td>Booked after the ambulatory EEG</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>PROMIS sleep-related impairment</td><td>At baseline and after treatment</td><td>Up to date</td><td>T-score 68 two weeks ago; repeat at the results visit</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Antiseizure medicine</td><td>Only if spells prove epileptic</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>Not started; awaiting the ambulatory EEG</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Headache diary (headache days and acute-medicine days)</td><td>Daily</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Started two weeks ago and reviewed today</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Acute headache medicine on no more than 2 days a week</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>No ibuprofen since the first visit; acetaminophen within the limit</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Headache days per three months</td><td>At baseline and 2 to 3 months after starting prevention</td><td>Up to date</td><td>54 days in the three months before intake; reassess at the three-month review</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Blood pressure, heart rate, and weight</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>140/84 mmHg, pulse 70, weight 52 kg; baseline before candesartan</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Basic metabolic panel (potassium and creatinine) on candesartan</td><td>Before starting and a few weeks after; then periodically</td><td>Up to date</td><td>Baseline two weeks ago: potassium 4.2, creatinine 0.84, eGFR 72. Recheck in four weeks</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Safety advice for sudden sleep episodes</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Reviewed with her today; she does not drive</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Depression screen (PHQ-9)</td><td>At baseline and at follow-up</td><td>Up to date</td><td>Score 3 at intake; repeat at the three-month review</td></tr></tbody></table><paragraph>Goal progress:</paragraph><list><item>Stop daily over-the-counter analgesics and limit acute headache medicine to no more than 2 days a week. In progress; target 2026-05-18 [S]</item><item>Cut headache days by at least half by the three-month review. In progress; target 2026-08-17 [S]</item><item>Reduce daytime sleep attacks and lower the PROMIS sleep-related impairment T-score below 60. In progress; target 2026-08-17 [S]</item><item>Establish whether the spells are epileptic before considering antiseizure medicine. In progress; target 2026-05-18 [S]</item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: Evidence-based guideline: Management of an unprovoked first seizure in adults (Krumholz A, et al. Neurology 2015;84(16):1705-1713) (2015); Treatment of central disorders of hypersomnolence: an American Academy of Sleep Medicine clinical practice guideline (Maski K, et al. J Clin Sleep Med 2021;17(9):1881-1893) (2021); The International Classification of Headache Disorders, 3rd edition (Cephalalgia 2018;38(1):1-211) (2018); The American Headache Society Consensus Statement: Update on integrating new migraine treatments into clinical practice (Ailani J, et al. Headache 2021;61(7):1021-1039) (2021)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Ambulatory EEG, then polysomnography and a multiple sleep latency test. Results visit with potassium and creatinine in four weeks. Next scheduled visit 2026-05-18: review of ambulatory EEG and sleep studies; four-week headache check. [S]</paragraph></text>

